    "better": {
      "word": "BETTER",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "Advantage, superiority, or victory; -- usually with of; as, to get the better of an enemy.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "One who has a claim to precedence; a superior, as in merit, social standing, etc.; -- usually in the plural. Their betters would hardly be found. Hooker. For the better, in the way of improvement; so as to produce improvement. \"If I have altered him anywhere for the better.\" Dryden.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "In a superior or more excellent manner; with more skill and wisdom, courage, virtue, advantage, or success; as, Henry writes better than John; veterans fight better than recruits. I could have better spared a better man. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adverb"
        },
        {
          "text": "More correctly or thoroughly. The better to understand the extent of our knowledge. Locke.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adverb"
        },
        {
          "text": "In a higher or greater degree; more; as, to love one better than another. Never was monarch better feared, and loved. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adverb"
        },
        {
          "text": "More, in reference to value, distance, time, etc.; as, ten miles and better. [Colloq.] To think better of (any one), to have a more favorable opinion of any one. and alter one's decision.",
          "partOfSpeech": "adverb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To improve or ameliorate; to increase the good qualities of. Love betters what is best. Wordsworth. He thought to better his circumstances. Thackeray.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To improve the condition of, morally, physically, financially, socially, or otherwise. The constant effort of every man to better himself. Macaulay.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To surpass in excellence; to exceed; to excel. The works of nature do always aim at that which can not be bettered. Hooker.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To give advantage to; to support; to advance the interest of. Weapons more violent, when next we meet, May serve to better us and worse our foes. Milton. reform; advance; promote.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To become better; to improve. Carlyle.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "One who bets or lays a wager.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
