    "between": {
      "word": "BETWEEN",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "In the space which separates; betwixt; as, New York is between Boston and Philadelphia.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Used in expressing motion from one body or place to another; from one to another of two. If things should go so between them. Bacon.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Belonging in common to two; shared by both. Castor and Pollux with only one soul between them. Locke.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Belonging to, or participated in by, two, and involving reciprocal action or affecting their mutual relation; as, opposition between science and religion. An intestine struggle, open or secret, between authority and liberty. Hume.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "With relation to two, as involved in an act or attribute of which another is the agent or subject; as, to judge between or to choose between courses; to distinguish between you and me; to mediate between nations.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "In intermediate relation to, in respect to time, quantity, or degree; as, between nine and ten o'clock. Between decks, the space, or in the space, between the decks of a vessel. confidence; with the understanding that the matter is not to be communicated to others. quarrel between two men or two nations; to be between two fires, etc. It is however extended to more than two in expressing a certain relation. I . . . hope that between public business, improving studies, and domestic pleasures, neither melancholy nor caprice will find any place for entrance. Johnson. Among implies a mass or collection of things or persons, and always supposes more than two; as, the prize money was equally divided among the ship's crew.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "Intermediate time or space; interval. [Poetic & R.] Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        }
      ],
      "relatedWords": []
    },
    "betwixt": {
      "word": "BETWIXT",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "In the space which separates; between. From betwixt two aged oaks. Milton.",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        },
        {
          "text": "From one to another of; mutually affecting. There was some speech of marriage Betwixt myself and her. Shak. Betwixt and between, in a midway position; so-so; neither one thing nor the other. [Colloq.]",
          "partOfSpeech": "preposition"
        }
      ],
      "relatedWords": []
    }
  }
